  "market_context": "After a strong bearish impulse move that broke prior structure, price retraced into the Fibonacci gold zone (0.5–0.618) on the 1-hour chart. The analyst demonstrated this as a repeatable short entry method applicable beyond indices.",
  "key_observation": "The analyst identified the impulsive bearish move that broke previous structure, then highlighted the retracement to the Fibonacci gold zone as the high-probability short entry area.",
  "analysis_steps": [
    "Step 1: Identify the impulsive bearish move that broke the previous market structure (top to bottom of the strongest non-stop bearish candles including wicks).",
    "Step 2: Draw Fibonacci retracement from the swing high (start of impulse) to swing low (end of impulse).",
    "Step 3: Locate the gold zone (0.5 to 0.618 retracement level) as the primary entry area for shorts.",
    "Step 4: Place take-profit at the previous structure high (not Fibonacci extension), citing reliability over extended targets.",
    "Step 5: Note that confluence with prior structure and rejection patterns (e.g., doji + engulfing) strengthens the signal."
  ],
  "outcome": null,
  "lessons": "Fibonacci retracements work best when drawn from a clear break-of-structure impulse move; the gold zone (0.5–0.618) is the highest-probability entry, and take-profit at prior structure is safer than extensions during volatile news periods.",
